    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d90">

                <pb facs="0136" n="126" xml:id="pg126"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 P.W./7–345: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Hurley</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-07-03T17:00:00+07:00">July 3, 1945—5
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received July 3—11:35 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1096. Department’s telegram 992, June 20 [<hi rendition="#italic">26</hi>], 3
                    p.m. This telegram sent to the Department and repeated to London and New
                    Delhi.</p>
                <p>Regarding the Chinese troops which were reported to have crossed the Thai border
                    near Mong Yawng. The Deputy Chief of Staff, U. S. Army Forces, China
                        Theater,<anchor n="56" xml:id="d90-f1"/> states that General Wedemeyer will
                    request the Generalissimo to have these Chinese troops withdrawn.</p>
                <p>The only information in the premises received by headquarters is the OSS report
                    referred to in the Department’s telegram.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hurley</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d90-f1" n="56" xml:id="d90-f1-content">
                <p>Maj. Gen. H. H. Fuller.</p>
